      "url": "https://www.last.fm/music/Swervedriver/Raise",
      "wiki_content": "Raise is the debut studio album by English alternative rock band Swervedriver. It was released on 30 September 1991 by Creation Records. The album contained six new songs; \"Son of Mustang Ford\", \"Rave Down\" and \"Sandblasted\" had all appeared on earlier EPs and singles released by the band between 1990 and 1991. The album received very positive reviews.\n\nEarly pressings of the UK LP came with a free 7\", which included the instrumental tracks \"Surf Twang\" (a 4-track version of \"Last Train to Satansville\") and \"Deep Twang\" (a 4-track version of \"Deep Seat\"). The album was reissued as a remastered and expanded edition with four bonus tracks in 2008 by Sony BMG in the United Kingdom and in 2009 by Hi-Speed Soul and Second Motion Records in the United States.\n\nIn 2016, Pitchfork ranked Raise at number 15 on its list of \"The 50 Best Shoegaze Albums of All Time\". <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Swervedriver/Raise\">Read more on Last.fm</a>. User-contributed text is available under the Creative Commons By-SA License; additional terms may apply.",
      "wiki_summary": "Raise is the debut studio album by English alternative rock band Swervedriver. It was released on 30 September 1991 by Creation Records. The album contained six new songs; \"Son of Mustang Ford\", \"Rave Down\" and \"Sandblasted\" had all appeared on earlier EPs and singles released by the band between 1990 and 1991. The album received very positive reviews. Early pressings of the UK LP came with a free 7\", which included the instrumental tracks \"Surf Twang\" (a 4-track version of \"Last Train to Satansville\") and \"Deep Twang\" (a 4-track version of \"Deep Seat\"). The album was reissued as a remastered <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Swervedriver/Raise\">Read more on Last.fm</a>."
    },
    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Swervedriver’s Raise is a landmark 1991 debut that gives shoegaze a harder, more kinetic edge. Built from scorching, pedal-heavy guitars, thunderous rhythms, and Adam Franklin’s cool, understated vocals, the album draws as much from Dinosaur Jr., Sonic Youth, Hüsker Dü, and American alternative rock as from the dreamier side of the British shoegaze scene. Songs such as “Son of Mustang Ford,” “Rave Down,” “Sci-Flyer,” and “Sandblasted” turn highway imagery, speed, and escape into a muscular, psychedelic form of guitar rock.\n\nInitially difficult to categorize, Raise has since earned recognition as one of the defining and most distinctive albums of its era. Its combination of grunge weight, art-rock dissonance, punk momentum, and expansive melodic atmosphere helped establish Swervedriver as a bridge between shoegaze and the alternative-rock boom that followed. This 2026 Sony Music reissue revisits the original album, preserving its sense of forward motion and reaffirming its lasting influence on guitar-driven rock.",
